We’re teaming up with @Google on NeuralGCM+ for faster, more reliable climate projections.

Multimillion-dollar, 3-year funding from https://Google.org & support from Google Research will expand our team—& we’ll open-source models & workflows. 🧵

https://allenai.org/blog/google-climate-partnership https://x.com/allen_ai/status/2118012880398209314/photo/1

@Google Google Research’s NeuralGCM, launched in 2024, uses physics to simulate large-scale atmospheric dynamics & AI for cloud formation & rainfall.

It can simulate historical climate, but reliable projections of a warmer future remain a research challenge.

@Google Since its release, Google Research has been developing NeuralGCM for weeks-to-months forecasts. Our focus will be years-to-decades climate projections, connecting atmosphere + ocean models to capture how oceans absorb & redistribute heat.

@Google We aim to answer questions like: how could rainfall & water availability change through mid-century in regions such as the southwestern U.S.?

Modeling the ocean & atmosphere together will enable exploring what that means for water supplies.

@Google Our Ai2 Climate Emulator (ACE) has already sped up climate simulations.

Our priorities for NeuralGCM+:

🌊 Couple atmosphere, ocean, & sea ice
🌡️ Add physics & test warmer climates
💻 Release models, frameworks, & documented workflows; improve GPU performance https://x.com/allen_ai/status/2118012885280457181/photo/1

@Google Our long-term ambition: a shared research platform climate modeling labs and scientists can use & improve together, helping communities plan for a changing climate.

For open-source collaboration platform @Nextclouders, local AI is only part of digital sovereignty. Users also need to inspect how a model was built.

That’s why Nextcloud chose Olmo 3 to power AI in Nextcloud Assistant & its other apps. 🧵

https://allenai.org/blog/nextcloud https://x.com/allen_ai/status/2117388019707642138/photo/1

@Nextclouders Nextcloud bundles Olmo 3 with llm2, its backend for organizations to run language models locally.

Via llm2 + Nextcloud’s application suite, admins can configure Olmo centrally so employees can summarize email threads, find facts in files, & more. https://x.com/allen_ai/status/2117388021741871215/photo/1

@Nextclouders Nextcloud also chose Olmo with consumer-grade hardware in mind. 

The team wanted a local model users could run without expensive, high-end machines.

@Nextclouders Olmo 3 earned Nextcloud’s highest Ethical AI rating: green. 

Green-rated models have open-source training & inference software, can be self-hosted, & have publicly available training data. https://x.com/allen_ai/status/2117388024191328703/photo/1

Today we're announcing support for university research at @Caltech, @OhioStateMed, @LSU, @IndianaUniv, and @UTHealthHouston to help advance breakthroughs across science and medicine.

As part of the support for university researchers, we’ll provide access to our latest Muse models, including Muse Wonder and Muse Spark; open weight models like Muse Glimmer and Muse Spark Open; computer vision models such as SAM, V-JEPA, and DINO; as well as SeamlessM4T, our multilingual communication model.

We’re excited to support these five universities as they use our models to accelerate the important work they’re doing.

Read more on our research blog: https://go.meta.me/47G6wV3

Here’s a look at the hard problems these five universities are tackling:

@Caltech: Expanding our partnership with Caltech to test whether large language models can accelerate innovative research in mathematics and physical sciences – pushing the boundaries of what AI can do in rigorous scientific domains

@OhioStateMed: Developing AI workflows to assist radiologists screening X-rays, building AI tools for earlier heart failure detection, and building clinician-guided AI tools for Alzheimer’s diagnosis.

@LSU: Implementing AI to improve non-contact infant tracheostomy monitoring, neurosurgical outcomes, VR training for medical professionals, and more.

@IndianaUniv: Applying open-weight models to improve maternal-newborn care and learning, with a focus on tools that work with limited connectivity and accommodate diverse cognitive needs.

@UTHealthHouston: Using Muse Glimmer, one of our smallest open-weight models, to support projects that improve treatment for brain cancer and agentic benchmarking in healthcare settings.

